    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm running an organic kale operation out in the PNW and I'm seeing some pretty significant nitrogen deficiency in the mid-season growth. My soil pH is sitting at about 6.2 right now and I've been doing regular compost applications, but it doesn't seem to be hitting the mark. I'm looking to boost N levels without causing a major spike in my pH. Would you recommend going with bone meal or blood meal for this? I need a recommendation based on the N-P-K impact and how it'll interact with the current acidity.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Given your soil pH of 6.2, blood meal would be your better choice as it provides a rapid nitrogen boost without significantly altering acidity, whereas bone meal is high in calcium and can raise your pH too fast. However, since you are experiencing deficiency after composting, you should also consider a water-soluble nitrogen source like ammonium sulfate to avoid any pH shifts. Applying a controlled amount of blood meal now will give you quick results for your mid-season kale growth without the buffering issues bone meal might cause. Always monitor your ammonium levels carefully with these fertilizers to prevent potential toxicity.",
